counterfactuals.output:
Swift carried out a 23-day Target of Opportunity monitoring program of the supergiant fast X-ray transient IGR J11215−5952, beginning several days before its predicted 9 February 2007 outburst and continuing well afterward. Frequent XRT snapshots—supplemented by BAT coverage—caught the event from its rise through the bright peak and subsequent decline. The main flare occurred essentially on schedule, confirming the source’s unusual periodic behavior.

The campaign showed that:

- the very bright phase detected by wide-field instruments lasted only hours to roughly a day;
- it was embedded in a much longer, highly structured episode of X-ray activity, with multiple peaks and large flux changes;
- detectable accretion persisted for several days—possibly up to about ten days—rather than being confined to the brief flare.

Thus, the “fast” outburst was mainly the brightest part of a broader accretion phase. The observations argued against an intrinsically hour-long mass-transfer event and instead favored prolonged accretion, strongly modulated by the neutron star’s passage through a dense, structured component of the supergiant wind—often interpreted as an equatorial wind or disk—plus smaller-scale wind clumping.
